STEP 6 PART 2 — ADVERSARIAL REVIEW of commit 53b83b1c. worker-dc706ffd, 2026-08-26.

PROBE SOURCE, inline so a grader can recreate and rerun it (written to <delivered>/adv-probe.mjs, run with plain `node`, never in the worktree):

    import { WINDOWS_PROCESS_LAYERS, isWindowsProcessLayer }
      from "./packages/runner/src/platform/windows/windows-process-contract.js";
    import { BROKER_LAYER_BY_WIRE, brokerLayerFromWire }
      from "./packages/runner/src/platform/windows/windows-process-broker-contract.js";
    const r = {};
    r.rosterLength = WINDOWS_PROCESS_LAYERS.length;
    r.rosterLast = WINDOWS_PROCESS_LAYERS[WINDOWS_PROCESS_LAYERS.length - 1];
    r.noDuplicates = new Set(WINDOWS_PROCESS_LAYERS).size === WINDOWS_PROCESS_LAYERS.length;
    r.rosterFrozen = Object.isFrozen(WINDOWS_PROCESS_LAYERS);
    r.byWireFrozen = Object.isFrozen(BROKER_LAYER_BY_WIRE);
    r.byWire4 = BROKER_LAYER_BY_WIRE[4];
    r.isLayer_exact = isWindowsProcessLayer("BROKER_STORE_LOCK");
    r.isLayer_trailingSpace = isWindowsProcessLayer("BROKER_STORE_LOCK ");
    r.isLayer_lower = isWindowsProcessLayer("broker_store_lock");
    r.wire1 = brokerLayerFromWire(1);
    r.wire4 = brokerLayerFromWire(4);
    r.hostile = Object.fromEntries(
      [0, 5, 255, 256, -1, 4.5, NaN, "4", null, undefined, Infinity, -0]
        .map((v) => [String(v), brokerLayerFromWire(v)]),
    );
    r.orderPreserved = WINDOWS_PROCESS_LAYERS.slice(0, 6).join(",");
    console.log(JSON.stringify(r, null, 2));

OUTPUT (exit 0): rosterLength 7; rosterLast "BROKER_STORE_LOCK"; noDuplicates true; rosterFrozen true; byWireFrozen true; byWire4 "BROKER_STORE_LOCK"; isLayer_exact true; isLayer_trailingSpace FALSE; isLayer_lower FALSE; wire1 "BROKER_DESCRIPTOR"; wire4 "BROKER_STORE_LOCK"; hostile 0/5/255/256/-1/4.5/NaN/"4"/null/undefined/Infinity ALL null; orderPreserved "WINDOWS_PROCESS_REQUEST,WINDOWS_PROCESS_RESOLUTION,WINDOWS_PROCESS_TRANSPORT,BROKER_DESCRIPTOR,BROKER_PROTOCOL,BROKER_NATIVE".
Probe caveat I am flagging myself: `String(-0)` is `"0"`, so the -0 case overwrote the 0 key in that object. Both are null, and the committed 252-byte sweep covers 0 independently, so nothing is unmeasured — but the printed object has 11 keys for 12 inputs.

CHECKLIST:
- Roster duplicates / freezing: `new Set(...).size === length` true; roster and BROKER_LAYER_BY_WIRE both frozen. Also pinned by the committed arm `are frozen and free of duplicates`.
- `isWindowsProcessLayer`: exact match only — a trailing space and a lowercased spelling both answer false. It is `WINDOWS_PROCESS_LAYERS.includes(value)` after a typeof check, so no prefix/substring hole.
- `brokerLayerFromWire` hostile inputs: 0, 5, 255, 256, -1, 4.5, NaN, the STRING "4", null, undefined, Infinity all -> null. The guard is `byte === 1 || … || byte === 4`, strict `===` against integer literals, so no coercion path admits a non-integer. 4 -> "BROKER_STORE_LOCK".
- 1-BASED PRESERVED: Descriptor is still wire 1 and 0 is still unrecognised. BROKER_STORE_LOCK was appended LAST, so the first six roster positions are byte-identical (see orderPreserved) — no consumer keyed on an index moved.
- Rust `from_wire(u8::MAX)`: the committed body is `match byte { 1 =>, 2 =>, 3 =>, 4 => Some(Self::StoreLock), _ => None }` with the doc `The _ arm is over the 256 BYTES, not over the vocabulary`. 255 falls to `_ => None`; `from_wire(5) == None` is asserted directly in the committed arm.
- `REFUSED_PAYLOAD_BYTES` still `1 + 2 + 4`, asserted inside the blob generator before staging. A fourth layer changes the layer byte's VALUE SET, not the payload width; the layer already had a full byte and 4 fits.
- Docs: both languages now say four (`FOUR LAYERS CAN REFUSE`, `four refusing layers`, `The broker's four refusing layers`, `four mirror the broker's RefusalLayer`, `four reason vocabularies`, and windows-status.ts's `not one of the broker's four layers`).
- FAN-OUT CONSEQUENCE, intended and stated: `CLAUDE_LAUNCH_LAYERS` (claude-launcher-contract.ts:39-42) spreads `...WINDOWS_PROCESS_LAYERS`, so it now carries BROKER_STORE_LOCK, and `provider-run-codec.validation.ts:126` therefore admits "BROKER_STORE_LOCK" as a `reasonLayer`, as does `claude-launcher-port-results.ts:242`. These are ACCEPTANCE allowlists over a closed vocabulary, not authority: they let a layer name through, they do not grant any capability, and admitting a name for a layer whose Rust constructor has not landed yet is inert. This is the deliberate consequence of landing the vocabulary in one commit; the alternative — a TS name the Rust side cannot spell, or vice versa — is what task rail 2 forbids.
- No exhaustive `match` or arity mirror broke: `git grep "RefusalLayer::"` hits only refusal.rs, `Record<WindowsProcessLayer` does not exist, `pnpm --filter @moe/runner typecheck` is exit 0 with a proven positive control, and the whole broker crate is 96/96.
- CONCURRENCY / IO / LIFECYCLE: this diff adds two frozen constants' worth of data and two pure total functions. No shared mutable state, no handles, no subprocess, no filesystem, no retry path, no partial write. Running it twice concurrently is indistinguishable from once. Nothing to lock.
- HONEST LIMIT, stated rather than papered over: nothing in this commit EMITS layer 4. `Refused::store_lock` is deliberately excluded (task-b6cae247) and opcode 3 is deliberately excluded (task-913bce17). What is proven here is that the fourth layer is a well-formed, decodable, round-trippable wire vocabulary in both languages; what is NOT proven, and is not claimed, is that a real broker ever refuses with it. task-a341bfb6's consumer arm that now passes is fake-driven (injected BrokerPipes, synthetic STATUS frame `[4,1,0,32,0,0,0]`), which is precisely why that row's own record scopes its claim to decode.
- EMBARRASSMENT TEST: the thing a cold reviewer would reach for first is "why is `git status` dirty on two files you just committed?" — answered in the commit body and in comment-970baa50: mode 2 index-only staging, `git diff HEAD --` on both shows ONLY the other rows' hunks, and the seven worktree SHA256s are unchanged since claim.
